# Intent: touches on the face of the screen toggle mute, vision off and full quiet (owner design 2026-11-13): HOLD the mouth 0.5 s = mute on or off, TAP an eye = vision off or on, HOLD the forehead (above and between the eyes) 0.5 s = full quiet on or off. maipai_touch_core.h is plain C (tested on the host by tests/firmware/touch_core_test.c): the screen is tiled into edge margins (28 px top, 20 px sides and bottom, where the quick-settings, volume and stop swipes start), forehead, two eyes, mouth and the rest; a touch only counts if the finger stays within 10 px of where it went down, stays in the region it began in, and is a tap (400 ms or less) or a hold (500 ms or more); a touch that begins in an edge margin is never a toggle; anything that strays is a drag. A hold acts while the finger is still down. The toggle regions and the margins never start a listen (a tap on the rest of the face still does, as before), and no listen starts while muted or in full quiet. The toggle is silent: a short blink and the new look, no sound. ApplyPrivacyFace draws the looks (a flat sealed mouth for mute, half-lidded eyes for vision off, the sleeping face with a small pause mark for full quiet; the mint shapes are patch 108), keeps them through emotion changes (the sleeping face holds against every other emotion) and the saved state is drawn once at boot; a combined state combines the parts. Face unmute is refused when the hub turned off local unmute.
